@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Feeds") {
    The feed commands are the whole management surface: `add` writes a row, `remove` deletes the row
    the account it is given owns, and `list` answers one listing per run. The order `add` reads in is
    the guard, then the absolute-URL rule, then the duplicate read, then the category read, so a
    duplicate is reported before the category is resolved, and the url written is the url as the
    command read it.

    ```sh
    rivulet add https://example.com/feed.xml --user alice
    rivulet add https://example.com/feed.xml --category news --user alice
    rivulet remove https://example.com/feed.xml --user alice
    rivulet list
    rivulet list --categories
    rivulet refresh
    rivulet status --json
    ```

    A category is resolved from the title the flag names, or from the default when no title is named,
    and the account is resolved from `--user` or from the single account. `list` with no flag still
    means the feeds: the flag names which read the run makes, and a run that names both listing flags
    is reported as the rule the port names rather than as a choice the run picks.

    The listing order is the port's byte order, with the lowercased title as the tiebreaker, and each
    line carries the stored position and the stored admin flag rather than the schema default. The
    counts `status --json` reports are read over every row whatever its state, and a row with no
    stamp answers `null`.

    The round that writes entries is the cadence loop's own. The loop waits before its first read,
    takes one read per wait with the interval it was built with, reports a failing read and goes on,
    and reads nothing after the seam answers stop. `refresh` is the manual pass over the same batch:
    each job of the batch runs once, and a failure is reported without stopping the rest.

    One write transaction is taken per feed refresh, in WAL mode, and the storage layer takes its
    pragmas through its own wrapper, so nothing here calls `fsync` per row. The entry loop writes
    nothing for an entry the document no longer declares and for a tombstoned pair, which is the half
    that keeps a feed's rows from growing back.

    Retention reads the age as whole days: a zero age clamps to one day, and the pass takes the batch
    it is given and leaves the rows below the limit for the next run. Search runs through the FTS5
    index, which is the index the schema declares for the entry table.
  }
}
